Jonadab the Unsightly One <jonadab.theunsightlyone@gmail.com> wrote:
> On Tuesday, November 19, 2013 7:24:41 AM UTC-5, ais523 wrote:
>> > - When my character was XP 1, she found a kitten on level 3. 
>> > - She also found a gray ooze on level 3.
>> 
>> These are both symptoms of a change from NitroHack that I'm not sure
>> about, which makes the character's level have no effect on monster
>> generation. 
>
> I'm not sure about that one either.  My instinct suggests
> going in the opposite direction, and taking into account
> not only the player's XP level but also his attributes,
> such as strength and (naked) AC.

Me, too. I think there is a reason that monster scale with the player
experience level. It might not be realistic but in cases like these
IMO gameplay reasons trump realism.

BTW, the commit in question is this one:
"make level generation independent of the player's level + depth"
https://github.com/DanielT/NitroHack/commit/98a3b471ab8432680b5be886adc208aaf25bd867

>> It's not easy to outright change back to 3.4.3 behaviour,
>> unfortunately, given the way that NitroHack's engine works. 
>
> If the levels are all generated ahead of time, before the player
> gets there, I would say that this is fair.  (The main reason 
> vanilla never did it that way is that vanilla was optimized 
> out the royal wazooty so it could run on a baked clay abacus.)

This is hardly the only or most important reason. Pregenerating the
whole dungeon is unnecessary and if you want to take in the player's
experience level, it's actually easier to do it on the fly than
adjusting the pregenerated level the first time the player enters it.

Then there's issues if one of the major demon has been summoned by the
player before entering its level. Or what about artifacts that have
been randomly generated on one level, they would interfere with
artifact wishing.

What are the advantages of pregenerating the dungeon?

